{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-109772" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nr(y,\\theta )=⟨{e}^{1-{y}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y,\\,\\text{_______}⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $r(y,\\theta )$ above parametrizes the surface of revolution generated by rotating the curve $x={e}^{1-{y}^{2}}$ by $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis for $y\\in [\\frac{1}{2},2].$ What is the third component of $r?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081523/state.json" "file_sha256" "df928206f53cc1870397b9646c0a90601ead323bbc2514a026c3169c6caf5563"} {"question" "q-109772" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081523/assets/be6fb27c0d050a7007e9f4a0d526daaff40b8f7d01f20c63ac8cc00074121f28.png)\n\n \n\nA point $(x,y,z)$ on the surface generated by rotating the graph of $x=f(y)$ by $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis has the position vector\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨f(y)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y,\\,f(y)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩,\\,y\\in [a,b],\\,\\theta =[0,2\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that $x=f(y)={e}^{1-{y}^{2}}\\ge 0$ for all $y\\in [\\frac{1}{2},2].$ \n\nSo, the parametrization of our surface of revolution is \n\n$$\nr(y,\\theta )=⟨{e}^{1-{y}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y,\\,{e}^{1-{y}^{2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩,\\,y\\in [\\frac{1}{2},2],\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, the third component of $r$ is ${e}^{1-{y}^{2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081523/state.json" "file_sha256" "df928206f53cc1870397b9646c0a90601ead323bbc2514a026c3169c6caf5563"} {"question" "q-109772" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081523/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "07da1e988de4dec44e1f9ddd869ff32edf00b948689aa86d99f7e7341db10799"} {"question" "q-109772" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081523/state.json" "file_sha256" "df928206f53cc1870397b9646c0a90601ead323bbc2514a026c3169c6caf5563"} {"question" "q-109772" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2\\pi {e}^{1-{y}^{2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta "] ["math" "2\\pi {e}^{1-{y}^{2}}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta "] ["math" "\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta "] ["math" "\\pi {e}^{1-{y}^{2}}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta "] ["math" "{e}^{1-{y}^{2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta "]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081523/state.json" "file_sha256" "df928206f53cc1870397b9646c0a90601ead323bbc2514a026c3169c6caf5563"} {"question" "q-109772" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{e}^{1-{y}^{2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta "] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081523/state.json" "file_sha256" "df928206f53cc1870397b9646c0a90601ead323bbc2514a026c3169c6caf5563"} {"question" "q-161051" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n⟨\\,(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\text{_______}\\,,\\,(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩\n$$\n\n \n\nThe fundamental vector product of the surface of revolution generated by rotating the curve $x=1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$ by $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis for $y\\in [0,\\pi ]$ is given above. What is the $2$nd component of the product?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081523/state.json" "file_sha256" "df928206f53cc1870397b9646c0a90601ead323bbc2514a026c3169c6caf5563"} {"question" "q-161051" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Notice that $x=1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\ge 0$ for all $y\\in [0,\\pi ].$ The parametrization of our surface of revolution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨f(y)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,y,\\,f(y)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩,\\,(y,\\theta )\\in D\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)=1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y,$ and \n\n$$\nD={(y,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{y}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂y},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂y},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂y}⟩=⟨{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,1,\\,{f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\ {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂\\theta }⟩=⟨-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0,\\,f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{y}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ {f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 0 & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 1 \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 0\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-{f}^{′}f{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{f}^{′}f{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ,\\,f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-{f}^{′}f,\\,f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f=1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y,$ we obtain ${f}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.$ So, finally, we conclude that the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{y}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}=⟨(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y),\\,(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081523/state.json" "file_sha256" "df928206f53cc1870397b9646c0a90601ead323bbc2514a026c3169c6caf5563"} {"question" "q-161051" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081523/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "07da1e988de4dec44e1f9ddd869ff32edf00b948689aa86d99f7e7341db10799"} {"question" "q-161051" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081523/state.json" "file_sha256" "df928206f53cc1870397b9646c0a90601ead323bbc2514a026c3169c6caf5563"} {"question" "q-161051" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)"] ["math" "1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y"] ["math" "\\operatorname{cos}⁡y(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)"] ["math" "\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y"] ["math" "{cos}^{2}⁡y"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081523/state.json" "file_sha256" "df928206f53cc1870397b9646c0a90601ead323bbc2514a026c3169c6caf5563"} {"question" "q-161051" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081523/state.json" "file_sha256" "df928206f53cc1870397b9646c0a90601ead323bbc2514a026c3169c6caf5563"} {"question" "q-161076" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n⟨\\,\\text{_______}\\,,\\,-(x-2)^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,,\\,-(x-2)^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩\n$$\n\n \n\nThe fundamental vector product of the surface of revolution generated by rotating the curve $y=(x-2)^{2}$ by $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis for $x\\in [0,2]$ is given above. What is the $1$st  component of the product?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081523/state.json" "file_sha256" "df928206f53cc1870397b9646c0a90601ead323bbc2514a026c3169c6caf5563"} {"question" "q-161076" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Notice that $y=(x-2)^{2}\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in [0,2].$ The parametrization of our surface of revolution is  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨x,f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩,\\,(x,\\theta )\\in D\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)=(x-2)^{2},$ and  \n\n$$\nD={(x,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂x},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂x},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂x}⟩=⟨1,\\,{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,{f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\ {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂\\theta }⟩=⟨0,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨f{f}^{′}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +f{f}^{′}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨f{f}^{′},\\,-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f=(x-2)^{2},$ we obtain ${f}^{′}=2(x-2).$ So, finally, we conclude that the fundamental vector product is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}=⟨2(x-2)^{3},\\,-(x-2)^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-(x-2)^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081523/state.json" "file_sha256" "df928206f53cc1870397b9646c0a90601ead323bbc2514a026c3169c6caf5563"} {"question" "q-161076" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081523/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "07da1e988de4dec44e1f9ddd869ff32edf00b948689aa86d99f7e7341db10799"} {"question" "q-161076" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081523/state.json" "file_sha256" "df928206f53cc1870397b9646c0a90601ead323bbc2514a026c3169c6caf5563"} {"question" "q-161076" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(x-2)^{2}"] ["math" "2(x-2)^{3}"] ["math" "4(x-2)"] ["math" "\\frac{2}{(x-2)^{3}}"] ["math" "x"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081523/state.json" "file_sha256" "df928206f53cc1870397b9646c0a90601ead323bbc2514a026c3169c6caf5563"} {"question" "q-161076" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2(x-2)^{3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081523/state.json" "file_sha256" "df928206f53cc1870397b9646c0a90601ead323bbc2514a026c3169c6caf5563"} {"question" "q-161091" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use a fundamental vector product to determine which of the following integrals gives the surface area generated when the curve $x={y}^{3}+1$ is rotated by $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis for $y\\in [0,1].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081523/state.json" "file_sha256" "df928206f53cc1870397b9646c0a90601ead323bbc2514a026c3169c6caf5563"} {"question" "q-161091" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Notice that $x={y}^{3}+1\\ge 0$ for all $y\\in [0,1].$ The parametrization of our surface of revolution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨f(y)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y,\\,f(y)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩,\\,(y,\\theta )\\in D\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)={y}^{3}+1,$ and\n\n$$\nD={(y,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{y}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂y},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂y}\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂y}⟩=⟨{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,1,\\,{f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\ {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂\\theta }⟩=⟨-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0,\\,f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{y}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ {f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 0 & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 1 \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 0\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f{f}^{′}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -f{f}^{′}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ,\\,f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f{f}^{′},\\,f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{y}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}‖ & =\\sqrt{(f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(-f{f}^{′})^{2}+(f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{f}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )+{f}^{2}({f}^{′})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{f}^{2}+{f}^{2}({f}^{′})^{2}} \\\\  & =f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}‖{r}_{y}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}‖\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\cdot \\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(y)={y}^{3}+1,$ we obtain ${f}^{′}(y)=3{y}^{2}.$ So, finally, we conclude that our expression for the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({y}^{3}+1)\\sqrt{1+(3{y}^{2})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({y}^{3}+1)\\sqrt{1+9{y}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081523/state.json" "file_sha256" "df928206f53cc1870397b9646c0a90601ead323bbc2514a026c3169c6caf5563"} {"question" "q-161091" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081523/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "07da1e988de4dec44e1f9ddd869ff32edf00b948689aa86d99f7e7341db10799"} {"question" "q-161091" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081523/state.json" "file_sha256" "df928206f53cc1870397b9646c0a90601ead323bbc2514a026c3169c6caf5563"} {"question" "q-161091" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({y}^{3}+1)\\sqrt{1+3{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y"] ["math" "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({y}^{3}+1)\\sqrt{1+9{y}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}y"] ["math" "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({y}^{3}+1)^{2}\\sqrt{1+3{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y"] ["math" "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({y}^{3}+1)^{2}\\sqrt{1+9{y}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}y"] ["math" "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}3{y}^{2}\\sqrt{2+{y}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}y"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081523/state.json" "file_sha256" "df928206f53cc1870397b9646c0a90601ead323bbc2514a026c3169c6caf5563"} {"question" "q-161091" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({y}^{3}+1)\\sqrt{1+9{y}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}y"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081523/state.json" "file_sha256" "df928206f53cc1870397b9646c0a90601ead323bbc2514a026c3169c6caf5563"} {"question" "q-161094" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use a fundamental vector product to determine which of the following integrals gives the surface area generated when the curve $y={e}^{x}(x-1)$ is rotated by $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis for $x\\in [2,3].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081523/state.json" "file_sha256" "df928206f53cc1870397b9646c0a90601ead323bbc2514a026c3169c6caf5563"} {"question" "q-161094" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Notice that $y={e}^{x}(x-1)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in [2,3].$ The parametrization of our surface of revolution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨x,\\,f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩,\\,(x,\\theta )\\in D\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)={e}^{x}(x-1),$ and\n\n$$\nD={(x,\\theta )\\,:\\,2\\le x\\le 3,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂x},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂x},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂x}⟩=⟨1,\\,{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,{f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\ {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂\\theta }⟩=⟨0,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨f{f}^{′}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +f{f}^{′}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨f{f}^{′},\\,-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}‖ & =\\sqrt{(f{f}^{′})^{2}+(-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{f}^{2}({f}^{′})^{2}+{f}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{f}^{2}({f}^{′})^{2}+{f}^{2}} \\\\  & =f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}‖{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}‖\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\cdot \\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{3}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)={e}^{x}(x-1),$ we obtain ${f}^{′}(x)=x{e}^{x}.$ So, finally, we conclude that our expression for the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{3}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{3}{e}^{x}(x-1)\\sqrt{1+(x{e}^{x})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{3}{e}^{x}(x-1)\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}{e}^{2x}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081523/state.json" "file_sha256" "df928206f53cc1870397b9646c0a90601ead323bbc2514a026c3169c6caf5563"} {"question" "q-161094" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081523/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "07da1e988de4dec44e1f9ddd869ff32edf00b948689aa86d99f7e7341db10799"} {"question" "q-161094" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081523/state.json" "file_sha256" "df928206f53cc1870397b9646c0a90601ead323bbc2514a026c3169c6caf5563"} {"question" "q-161094" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{3}{e}^{x}(x+1)\\sqrt{1+(x-1)^{2}{e}^{2x}}\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "2\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{3}{e}^{x}(x+1)\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}{e}^{2x}}\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "2\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{3}{e}^{x}(x-1)\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}{e}^{2x}}\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{2}^{3}{e}^{x}(x+1)\\sqrt{1+(x-1)^{2}{e}^{2x}}\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{2}^{3}{e}^{x}(x-1)\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}{e}^{2x}}\\,\\text{d}x"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081523/state.json" "file_sha256" "df928206f53cc1870397b9646c0a90601ead323bbc2514a026c3169c6caf5563"} {"question" "q-161094" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{3}{e}^{x}(x-1)\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}{e}^{2